In $RR^3$, vector addition composes position vectors along the parallelogram spanned by $bold(a)$ and $bold(b)$, and $lambda bold(a)$ for $lambda in RR$ lies on the line $O A$ with length $abs(lambda bold(a)) = abs(lambda) abs(bold(a))$. In $RR^n$ both operations act componentwise:
$ bold(a) + bold(b) = (a_1 + b_1, ..., a_n + b_n), quad lambda bold(a) = (lambda a_1, ..., lambda a_n). $
The same componentwise definitions make $CC^n$ a vector space over $CC$; restricting scalars to $RR$, it is a real vector space of dimension $2n$.
